{
  "id": "py-06-types",
  "track": "python",
  "unit": "py-u1",
  "order": 6,
  "title": "Types and converting",
  "summary": "Every value has a type. Turn text into numbers and numbers into text on purpose.",
  "language": "python",
  "minutes": 20,
  "visual": "memory",
  "visuals": [
    "types",
    "expr"
  ],
  "intro": {
    "text": "Every value has a **type**: `7` is an **int**, `2.5` is a **float**, `\"hi\"` is a **str**. `type(x)` tells you which.\n\nPython never mixes text and numbers by itself. `\"3\" + 4` is an error, because Python cannot know if you meant 7 or \"34\". You convert on purpose:\n\n- `int(\"42\")` turns text into a whole number, 42\n- `float(\"2.5\")` turns text into a decimal number, 2.5\n- `str(42)` turns a number into text, \"42\"\n\n`int()` only accepts text that is a whole number: `int(\"hi\")` and `int(\"3.5\")` are errors. Given a float, `int()` just drops the decimals (`int(3.9)` is 3). To round to the nearest whole number, use `round()`.",
    "analogy": "Types are like different currencies. You cannot pay with euros in a shop that only takes dollars, even if the number is the same. You change the money first, on purpose, at the exchange: int(), float() and str() are the exchange desks."
  },
